  "result": "**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` only accepted plain digits like `12.50`. Prices with thousands separators, such as `\"1,005.99\"`, didn't match its regex. The code then read `m[1]` on a `null` match and threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`, which surfaced as a 500.\n\nThe log shows 37 errors on `POST /checkout`. All 37 came from three price strings: `1,005.99`, `1,299.00` and `2,450.50`. No other price format in the log failed.\n\n**Fix:**\n- `parseAmount` now accepts properly grouped thousands (`1,005.99`, `1,000,000`) and strips the commas before converting to cents.\n- Malformed input (`abc`, `1,00`, `-5`, `1.234`) now throws the documented `Error('Invalid amount: …')` instead of a `TypeError`.\n- Those malformed inputs are still rejected, so checkout still fails on them, but with a clear message. The handler in `src/server.js` could turn that into a 400 instead of a 500. I didn't change it.\n\n**Tests** (added to `test/money.test.js`):\n- Comma-formatted amounts parse correctly.\n- Malformed amounts throw a descriptive `Error`.\n- `checkout` totals correctly for a comma-formatted price.\n\n`npm test` passes with 7 of 7 tests. I didn't run the new tests against the old code, but the first and third would have failed there, because the old code threw a `TypeError` on any comma.\n\nI haven't committed anything.",
